- Family type: Joint family may provide more social support, potentially reducing risk; nuclear family might increase isolation.
- Total children: More children may increase stress and risk; first-time mothers might experience anxiety but not necessarily depression.
- Number of household members: More household members might indicate more support or more stress depending on dynamics.
- Birth compliancy: Complications during birth can increase risk of postpartum depression due to trauma or health concerns.
- Relationship between father and newborn: A good or very good relationship may reduce risk; neutral or bad may increase risk.
- Age: Younger or older maternal age might influence risk; very young or older mothers might have higher risk.
- Trust and share feelings: Being able to trust and share feelings is protective; inability increases risk.
- Education level: Higher education might be protective due to better access to resources; lower education might increase risk.
- Pregnancy plan: Unplanned pregnancy is a known risk factor for postpartum depression.

